{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015jBiStijLfymkrM1MjBBDS","type":"tool_result","content":"package state\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"database/sql\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/state/sqlitex\"\n)\n\n// Event kinds.\nconst (\n\tEventClaim    = \"claim\"\n\tEventCloseout = \"closeout\"\n\tEventFailure  = \"failure\"\n\tEventRetry    = \"retry\"\n\t// EventUsageLimit: a job stopped at a usage limit. It is not a failure.\n\tEventUsageLimit = \"usage_limit\"\n\t// EventKill: the Reaper is about to kill a running job; data has the reason.\n\tEventKill      = \"kill\"\n\tEventTickError = \"tick_error\"\n\t// EventHostUntrusted: a host-mode job of a role that reads untrusted\n\t// input started. Stage, role, job and issue are in their columns.\n\tEventHostUntrusted = \"host_untrusted\"\n\t// EventGate: the triage fan-in gate acted; data has verdict, reason and worker.\n\tEventGate = \"gate\"\n\t// EventMerge: the merger squash-merged a PR; data has pr, head and merge_sha.\n\tEventMerge = \"merge\"\n\t// EventReviewRound: a review fix round changed phase; data has round, phase, reason, pr, head, worker and roles.\n\tEventReviewRound = \"review_round\"\n)\n\n// Event is one row of the append-only event log. Data holds structured fields\n// only: never log text, issue bodies or secrets.\ntype Event struct {\n\tID    int64\n\tAt    time.Time\n\tKind  string\n\tJobID string\n\tIssue int\n\tStage string\n\tRole  string\n\tData  json.RawMessage\n}\n\n// EventFilter selects events; zero values are unbounded.\ntype EventFilter struct {\n\tSince, Until time.Time\n\tKind         string\n\tIssue        int    // 0 = any\n\tStage, Role  string // \"\" = any\n\tLimit        int\n}\n\ntype execer interface {\n\tExecContext(ctx context.Context, q string, args ...any) (sql.Result, error)\n}\n\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"append event %s: %w\", e.Kind, err)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// AppendEvent appends e; a zero At means now.\nfunc (s *Store) AppendEvent(ctx context.Context, e Event) error {\n\tif e.Kind == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"append event: empty kind\")\n\t}\n\tif e.At.IsZero() {\n\t\te.At = s.now()\n\t}\n\treturn insertEvent(ctx, s.db, e)\n}\n\n// Events returns matching events in ascending id order.\nfunc (s *Store) Events(ctx context.Context, f EventFilter) ([]Event, error) {\n\tq := `SELECT id, ts, kind, job_id, issue, stage, role, data FROM events WHERE 1=1`\n\tvar args []any\n\tif !f.Since.IsZero() {\n\t\tq += ` AND ts >= ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Since.Unix())\n\t}\n\tif !f.Until.IsZero() {\n\t\tq += ` AND ts <= ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Until.Unix())\n\t}\n\tif f.Kind != \"\" {\n\t\tq += ` AND kind = ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Kind)\n\t}\n\tif f.Issue != 0 {\n\t\tq += ` AND issue = ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Issue)\n\t}\n\tif f.Stage != \"\" {\n\t\tq += ` AND stage = ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Stage)\n\t}\n\tif f.Role != \"\" {\n\t\tq += ` AND role = ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Role)\n\t}\n\tq += ` ORDER BY id`\n\tif f.Limit > 0 {\n\t\tq += ` LIMIT ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Limit)\n\t}\n\trows, err := s.db.QueryContext(ctx, q, args...)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"query events: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tdefer rows.Close()\n\tvar out []Event\n\tfor rows.Next() {\n\tif c == nil || c.Intervals.Merger <= 0 {\n\t\treturn defaultInterval\n\t}\n\treturn time.Duration(c.Intervals.Merger)\n}\n\n// TickTimeout implements controller.TickTimeouter.\nfunc (*Loop) TickTimeout(*config.Resolved) time.Duration { return 5 * time.Minute }\n\n// Claims implements controller.Loop: the loop is skipped while paused.\nfunc (*Loop) Claims() bool { return true }\n\n// tick holds what one Tick passes between its steps.\ntype tick struct {\n\tl        *Loop\n\tcfg      *config.Resolved\n\tlog      *slog.Logger\n\tself     string\n\tdry      bool\n\tn        uint64\n\tstopping <-chan struct{}\n\n\trequired []github.RequiredCheck // empty: no rules apply\n\tmainTip  string\n\tbusy     map[int]bool // issues with a running worker or review job\n\tupdated  bool         // one branch update per tick\n\terrs     []error\n}\n\n// Tick implements controller.Loop.\nfunc (l *Loop) Tick(ctx context.Context, env controller.Env) error {\n\td := l.d\n\tlog := env.Log\n\tif log == nil {\n\t\tlog = d.Log\n\t}\n\tif env.Cfg == nil || env.Cfg.SHA == \"\" {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tid, err := d.Reader.Identity(ctx)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"merge: identify github user: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tl.mu.Lock()\n\tl.ticks++\n\tn := l.ticks\n\tl.mu.Unlock()\n\tt := &tick{l: l, cfg: env.Cfg, log: log, self: id.Login, dry: d.Effects.DryRun(), n: n, stopping: env.Stopping}\n\n\tprs, err := t.candidates(ctx)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tl.forget(prs)\n\tif len(prs) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tif err := t.prepare(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tfor _, pr := range prs {\n\t\tif ctx.Err() != nil || t.isStopping() {\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t\tif t.consider(ctx, pr) {\n\t\t\tbreak // merged: main moved\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn errors.Join(t.errs...)\n}\n\nfunc (t *tick) isStopping() bool {\n\tselect {\n\tcase <-t.stopping:\n\t\treturn true\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n}\n\n// candidates lists the open worker PRs of the App on main, oldest first.\nfunc (t *tick) candidates(ctx context.Context) ([]github.PullRequest, error) {\n\tprs, err := t.l.d.Reader.ListPRs(ctx, github.ListPRsOpts{State: github.StateOpen, Base: \"main\"})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"merge: list PRs: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tvar out []github.PullRequest\n\tfor _, pr := range prs {\n\t\tif pr.State != github.StateOpen || pr.Merged || pr.Draft || pr.BaseRef != \"main\" || !strings.EqualFold(pr.Author, t.self) {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif _, _, ok := parseBranch(pr.HeadRef); !ok {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tout = append(out, pr)\n\t}\n## Merger loop\n\nThe merger is controller code, not an agent, and takes no job slot. Every 5 minutes it merges at most one PR that has cleared every gate, oldest first, and the merge closes the issue. It also updates at most one behind branch per tick, so CI capacity goes to the next merge rather than to churn. **The merger is the gate, not GitHub:** the App bypasses `main`'s whole ruleset, so GitHub would accept a merge with red or pending checks. Every gate is checked in code and every unreadable fact fails closed.\n\nOnly ready (non-draft) PRs the App opened on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` with base `main` are candidates. The reviewer loop marks a PR ready, but the merger re-checks everything itself and never trusts the draft flag, because a person can mark a PR ready by hand. A PR merges when all of these hold:\n\n- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does). A stale review is only logged: a person must re-request it; fix rounds rerun reviewers only after a worker fix\n- it carries none of `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:tests:failed` or `ard:executor:failed`, its issue is open and carries neither `ard:hold` nor `ard:needs-human`, and no worker or review job of the issue is running\n- the first line of the PR body is exactly `Closes #<issue>` (what the worker writes; it is what closes the issue) and the state records a worker job of the issue on this branch (its id is the `Ard-Job` trailer). Otherwise the PR gets `ard:needs-human`\n- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n- the branch is up to date with `main`; if it is behind, the merger updates it and checks again next tick on the new head. \"Behind\" means `main` changed something **outside `.ard/<digits>/`** since the merge base: the committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` to `main` on every close-out, and counting those would keep every branch behind forever. A history with several merge bases (criss-cross merges) is skipped with a warning, because the choice would decide what counts as the PR's own change. GitHub's merge API guards the head SHA, not the base, so a push to `main` between the merger's fetch and the merge goes unseen\n- checks are green on the current head, read from check runs only (commit statuses are not read, because the App has no `statuses` permission). The required list comes from GitHub's branch rules API for `main` (`GET /repos/{o}/{r}/rules/branches/main`, metadata read), with no copy in config:\n  - when the rules are readable and name required checks, each must be completed with `success`, `neutral` or `skipped` on the head (the latest run per name; a rerun in flight hides the old result). Missing or running checks wait; a red one (failure, cancelled, timed out, action required) gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment naming the checks, until #41 adds fix rounds\n  - when the rules are unavailable (a free-plan private repository answers 403 \"upgrade\", or 404) or no required checks apply, there is no list to go by: if the head has no check runs the merger merges on reviews alone, but only after a grace period since it first saw the head (at least one tick and two minutes), so CI that has not queued yet is not missed; if it has check runs, every latest run per name must be green\n  - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json` (13 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\n\nA protected path needs a GitHub review in state `APPROVED`, from a user who is not an App and has write, maintain or admin permission, that stands at a commit the current head still covers (the same rule as for reviews). A later `CHANGES_REQUESTED` or `DISMISSED` review by the same person voids it, a plain comment does not. A triage user cannot push or merge on GitHub, so their approval does not authorise a change to `.github/`, config or agent instructions. The label is not the approval: `ard:needs-approval` is informational, the merger adds it and posts one comment per head, and removing it changes nothing.\n\nIt squash-merges with the PR title plus ` (#<pr>)` (cleaned: whitespace and control characters collapsed, skip-CI tokens defused, closing references neutralised, at most 72 characters) and one `Ard-Job: <id>` trailer line per worker job of the branch, guarded by the head SHA. It then deletes the branch, so `.ard/<issue#>/` and its logs land on `main` with the code, and the `Closes #<issue>` line closes the issue. A merge records a `merge` event in the state store.\n\n**Pause and dry-run.** The merger loop is skipped while the controller is paused: no merges and no branch updates. In `--dry-run` it runs the same gates, writes only to the effects ledger and logs `merge.would_merge` and `merge.would_update`. `ard serve --loops` can leave it out.\n\n**Branch protection and the bot.** The committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to `main` with `[skip ci]`, which neither a \"require a pull request\" rule nor a required status check can admit, so the App must be on the bypass list of `main`'s ruleset (mode Always). GitHub's bypass covers the whole ruleset, not single rules (found on Gate 2, #39). ard keeps that bypass narrow itself: the committer refuses any path outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, the merger merges only when every review passed and required checks are green on the current head (checked in code, not left to GitHub), and the bot-commit audit flags any bot commit on `main` that touches anything else. GitHub enforces nothing on the App's merges, so the merger reads the required checks itself from the branch rules API. A squash merge has GitHub (`noreply@github.com`) as committer and the App as author, so the audit, which keys on the committer, does not cover merges: the merger's gates do.\n\n## Configuration\n\nConfiguration is by convention: a role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. No prompt path is written anywhere; ard reads `.ard/<type>/<name>/prompt.txt`. Model credentials are host settings (`ARD_AGENT_AUTH`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the `ard serve` environment), not `config.yaml` keys, because a PR can change `config.yaml`.\n\n```\n.ard/\n├── config.yaml          # global settings and overrides only\n├── scanners/<name>/     # scan (executable) or prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n└── <issue#>/            # per-issue output and logs, written only by the committer\n```\n\n- A directory with `prompt.txt` is an agent role; a scanner directory with an executable `scan` is a deterministic scanner.\n- A `Dockerfile` builds the role's image; without one the local executor runs a host subprocess and the Kubernetes executor uses the default `ard-agent` image (agent CLIs, git, common linters). A role that reads untrusted input without one triggers the host-mode warning (see Executors and the Reaper).\n- Any other file in the directory (scripts, allowlists) is mounted read-only beside the prompt.\n\n```yaml\nmax_pods: 3                  # job slots shared by every loop\nexecutor: local              # local | k8s\nagent: claude                # default CLI for agent roles\nrequire_approval: true       # human adds ard:approved before a worker starts\nbudget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo, UTC day, blocks at spent >= cap; a role may set its own; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2\ninternal/reviewer/doc.go:54:// ard:tests:failed is set. It logs review.marked_ready; a failed MarkReady is\ninternal/reviewer/loop.go:47:\tMarkReady(ctx context.Context, n int) error\ninternal/reviewer/ready.go:86:\t\tcovered, _, err := prgate.Covers(ctx, d.Git, d.RepoDir, head, pr.HeadSHA, \"\", issue)\ninternal/reviewer/ready.go:96:\tif err := d.Effects.MarkReady(ctx, prNumber); err != nil {\ninternal/reviewer/ready.go:97:\t\tt.log.Warn(\"review.mark_ready_failed\", \"pr\", prNumber, \"issue\", issue, \"error\", err)\ninternal/reviewer/ready.go:101:\t\tt.log.Info(\"review.would_mark_ready\", \"pr\", prNumber, \"issue\", issue, \"head\", pr.HeadSHA)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5da09b9e-ada1-4827-812d-8cb6fabac0a7","uuid":"4e77ff63-5b60-450f-a3cd-3f6b408598ae","timestamp":"2026-11-23T18:59:16.172Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"package state\n\nimport (\n\t\"context\"\n\t\"database/sql\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/state/sqlitex\"\n)\n\n// Event kinds.\nconst (\n\tEventClaim    = \"claim\"\n\tEventCloseout = \"closeout\"\n\tEventFailure  = \"failure\"\n\tEventRetry    = \"retry\"\n\t// EventUsageLimit: a job stopped at a usage limit. It is not a failure.\n\tEventUsageLimit = \"usage_limit\"\n\t// EventKill: the Reaper is about to kill a running job; data has the reason.\n\tEventKill      = \"kill\"\n\tEventTickError = \"tick_error\"\n\t// EventHostUntrusted: a host-mode job of a role that reads untrusted\n\t// input started. Stage, role, job and issue are in their columns.\n\tEventHostUntrusted = \"host_untrusted\"\n\t// EventGate: the triage fan-in gate acted; data has verdict, reason and worker.\n\tEventGate = \"gate\"\n\t// EventMerge: the merger squash-merged a PR; data has pr, head and merge_sha.\n\tEventMerge = \"merge\"\n\t// EventReviewRound: a review fix round changed phase; data has round, phase, reason, pr, head, worker and roles.\n\tEventReviewRound = \"review_round\"\n)\n\n// Event is one row of the append-only event log. Data holds structured fields\n// only: never log text, issue bodies or secrets.\ntype Event struct {\n\tID    int64\n\tAt    time.Time\n\tKind  string\n\tJobID string\n\tIssue int\n\tStage string\n\tRole  string\n\tData  json.RawMessage\n}\n\n// EventFilter selects events; zero values are unbounded.\ntype EventFilter struct {\n\tSince, Until time.Time\n\tKind         string\n\tIssue        int    // 0 = any\n\tStage, Role  string // \"\" = any\n\tLimit        int\n}\n\ntype execer interface {\n\tExecContext(ctx context.Context, q string, args ...any) (sql.Result, error)\n}\n\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"append event %s: %w\", e.Kind, err)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// AppendEvent appends e; a zero At means now.\nfunc (s *Store) AppendEvent(ctx context.Context, e Event) error {\n\tif e.Kind == \"\" {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"append event: empty kind\")\n\t}\n\tif e.At.IsZero() {\n\t\te.At = s.now()\n\t}\n\treturn insertEvent(ctx, s.db, e)\n}\n\n// Events returns matching events in ascending id order.\nfunc (s *Store) Events(ctx context.Context, f EventFilter) ([]Event, error) {\n\tq := `SELECT id, ts, kind, job_id, issue, stage, role, data FROM events WHERE 1=1`\n\tvar args []any\n\tif !f.Since.IsZero() {\n\t\tq += ` AND ts >= ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Since.Unix())\n\t}\n\tif !f.Until.IsZero() {\n\t\tq += ` AND ts <= ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Until.Unix())\n\t}\n\tif f.Kind != \"\" {\n\t\tq += ` AND kind = ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Kind)\n\t}\n\tif f.Issue != 0 {\n\t\tq += ` AND issue = ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Issue)\n\t}\n\tif f.Stage != \"\" {\n\t\tq += ` AND stage = ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Stage)\n\t}\n\tif f.Role != \"\" {\n\t\tq += ` AND role = ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Role)\n\t}\n\tq += ` ORDER BY id`\n\tif f.Limit > 0 {\n\t\tq += ` LIMIT ?`\n\t\targs = append(args, f.Limit)\n\t}\n\trows, err := s.db.QueryContext(ctx, q, args...)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"query events: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tdefer rows.Close()\n\tvar out []Event\n\tfor rows.Next() {\n\tif c == nil || c.Intervals.Merger <= 0 {\n\t\treturn defaultInterval\n\t}\n\treturn time.Duration(c.Intervals.Merger)\n}\n\n// TickTimeout implements controller.TickTimeouter.\nfunc (*Loop) TickTimeout(*config.Resolved) time.Duration { return 5 * time.Minute }\n\n// Claims implements controller.Loop: the loop is skipped while paused.\nfunc (*Loop) Claims() bool { return true }\n\n// tick holds what one Tick passes between its steps.\ntype tick struct {\n\tl        *Loop\n\tcfg      *config.Resolved\n\tlog      *slog.Logger\n\tself     string\n\tdry      bool\n\tn        uint64\n\tstopping <-chan struct{}\n\n\trequired []github.RequiredCheck // empty: no rules apply\n\tmainTip  string\n\tbusy     map[int]bool // issues with a running worker or review job\n\tupdated  bool         // one branch update per tick\n\terrs     []error\n}\n\n// Tick implements controller.Loop.\nfunc (l *Loop) Tick(ctx context.Context, env controller.Env) error {\n\td := l.d\n\tlog := env.Log\n\tif log == nil {\n\t\tlog = d.Log\n\t}\n\tif env.Cfg == nil || env.Cfg.SHA == \"\" {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tid, err := d.Reader.Identity(ctx)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"merge: identify github user: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tl.mu.Lock()\n\tl.ticks++\n\tn := l.ticks\n\tl.mu.Unlock()\n\tt := &tick{l: l, cfg: env.Cfg, log: log, self: id.Login, dry: d.Effects.DryRun(), n: n, stopping: env.Stopping}\n\n\tprs, err := t.candidates(ctx)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tl.forget(prs)\n\tif len(prs) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\tif err := t.prepare(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tfor _, pr := range prs {\n\t\tif ctx.Err() != nil || t.isStopping() {\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t\tif t.consider(ctx, pr) {\n\t\t\tbreak // merged: main moved\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn errors.Join(t.errs...)\n}\n\nfunc (t *tick) isStopping() bool {\n\tselect {\n\tcase <-t.stopping:\n\t\treturn true\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn false\n\t}\n}\n\n// candidates lists the open worker PRs of the App on main, oldest first.\nfunc (t *tick) candidates(ctx context.Context) ([]github.PullRequest, error) {\n\tprs, err := t.l.d.Reader.ListPRs(ctx, github.ListPRsOpts{State: github.StateOpen, Base: \"main\"})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, fmt.Errorf(\"merge: list PRs: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tvar out []github.PullRequest\n\tfor _, pr := range prs {\n\t\tif pr.State != github.StateOpen || pr.Merged || pr.Draft || pr.BaseRef != \"main\" || !strings.EqualFold(pr.Author, t.self) {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif _, _, ok := parseBranch(pr.HeadRef); !ok {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tout = append(out, pr)\n\t}\n## Merger loop\n\nThe merger is controller code, not an agent, and takes no job slot. Every 5 minutes it merges at most one PR that has cleared every gate, oldest first, and the merge closes the issue. It also updates at most one behind branch per tick, so CI capacity goes to the next merge rather than to churn. **The merger is the gate, not GitHub:** the App bypasses `main`'s whole ruleset, so GitHub would accept a merge with red or pending checks. Every gate is checked in code and every unreadable fact fails closed.\n\nOnly ready (non-draft) PRs the App opened on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` with base `main` are candidates. The reviewer loop marks a PR ready, but the merger re-checks everything itself and never trusts the draft flag, because a person can mark a PR ready by hand. A PR merges when all of these hold:\n\n- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does). A stale review is only logged: a person must re-request it; fix rounds rerun reviewers only after a worker fix\n- it carries none of `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:tests:failed` or `ard:executor:failed`, its issue is open and carries neither `ard:hold` nor `ard:needs-human`, and no worker or review job of the issue is running\n- the first line of the PR body is exactly `Closes #<issue>` (what the worker writes; it is what closes the issue) and the state records a worker job of the issue on this branch (its id is the `Ard-Job` trailer). Otherwise the PR gets `ard:needs-human`\n- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n- the branch is up to date with `main`; if it is behind, the merger updates it and checks again next tick on the new head. \"Behind\" means `main` changed something **outside `.ard/<digits>/`** since the merge base: the committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` to `main` on every close-out, and counting those would keep every branch behind forever. A history with several merge bases (criss-cross merges) is skipped with a warning, because the choice would decide what counts as the PR's own change. GitHub's merge API guards the head SHA, not the base, so a push to `main` between the merger's fetch and the merge goes unseen\n- checks are green on the current head, read from check runs only (commit statuses are not read, because the App has no `statuses` permission). The required list comes from GitHub's branch rules API for `main` (`GET /repos/{o}/{r}/rules/branches/main`, metadata read), with no copy in config:\n  - when the rules are readable and name required checks, each must be completed with `success`, `neutral` or `skipped` on the head (the latest run per name; a rerun in flight hides the old result). Missing or running checks wait; a red one (failure, cancelled, timed out, action required) gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment naming the checks, until #41 adds fix rounds\n  - when the rules are unavailable (a free-plan private repository answers 403 \"upgrade\", or 404) or no required checks apply, there is no list to go by: if the head has no check runs the merger merges on reviews alone, but only after a grace period since it first saw the head (at least one tick and two minutes), so CI that has not queued yet is not missed; if it has check runs, every latest run per name must be green\n  - when the rules cannot be read (a 5xx, a network error, a permissions 403), nothing merges that tick. Rule types ard does not evaluate (`workflows`, `code_scanning`, `required_deployments`) are logged at warn\n- the diff touches no protected path; the list comes only from `merge.protected_paths`, with no floor in code (decided, #3), so a human who empties the list removes the gate. `ard init` proposes the defaults `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the four role trees (`.ard/scanners/`, `.ard/triage/`, `.ard/workers/`, `.ard/reviewers/`), `**/CODEOWNERS`, and the agent instructions a PR could plant to steer later agents: `CLAUDE.md`, `**/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `**/AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json` (13 entries; `**/X` already matches a root `X`, the bare entries are kept for clarity). An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix; any other entry is a doublestar glob. Matching is case-insensitive on the repo-relative path (macOS file systems are, so `Claude.md` and `.Claude/x` are protected). A prefix matches on a directory boundary (`.github/` matches `.github` and `.github/x`, never `.githubx`). A glob follows doublestar: `*` stays within one path segment and `**` crosses them, so a bare `CODEOWNERS` would match only the root file. Quote globs that start with `*` in YAML. The merger checks both names of a renamed file; a malformed path counts as protected. A PR that touches one gets `ard:needs-approval` and merges on the next poll once a person with write access approves the current head with a GitHub review (below).\n\nA protected path needs a GitHub review in state `APPROVED`, from a user who is not an App and has write, maintain or admin permission, that stands at a commit the current head still covers (the same rule as for reviews). A later `CHANGES_REQUESTED` or `DISMISSED` review by the same person voids it, a plain comment does not. A triage user cannot push or merge on GitHub, so their approval does not authorise a change to `.github/`, config or agent instructions. The label is not the approval: `ard:needs-approval` is informational, the merger adds it and posts one comment per head, and removing it changes nothing.\n\nIt squash-merges with the PR title plus ` (#<pr>)` (cleaned: whitespace and control characters collapsed, skip-CI tokens defused, closing references neutralised, at most 72 characters) and one `Ard-Job: <id>` trailer line per worker job of the branch, guarded by the head SHA. It then deletes the branch, so `.ard/<issue#>/` and its logs land on `main` with the code, and the `Closes #<issue>` line closes the issue. A merge records a `merge` event in the state store.\n\n**Pause and dry-run.** The merger loop is skipped while the controller is paused: no merges and no branch updates. In `--dry-run` it runs the same gates, writes only to the effects ledger and logs `merge.would_merge` and `merge.would_update`. `ard serve --loops` can leave it out.\n\n**Branch protection and the bot.** The committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to `main` with `[skip ci]`, which neither a \"require a pull request\" rule nor a required status check can admit, so the App must be on the bypass list of `main`'s ruleset (mode Always). GitHub's bypass covers the whole ruleset, not single rules (found on Gate 2, #39). ard keeps that bypass narrow itself: the committer refuses any path outside `.ard/<issue#>/`, the merger merges only when every review passed and required checks are green on the current head (checked in code, not left to GitHub), and the bot-commit audit flags any bot commit on `main` that touches anything else. GitHub enforces nothing on the App's merges, so the merger reads the required checks itself from the branch rules API. A squash merge has GitHub (`noreply@github.com`) as committer and the App as author, so the audit, which keys on the committer, does not cover merges: the merger's gates do.\n\n## Configuration\n\nConfiguration is by convention: a role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. No prompt path is written anywhere; ard reads `.ard/<type>/<name>/prompt.txt`. Model credentials are host settings (`ARD_AGENT_AUTH`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` in the `ard serve` environment), not `config.yaml` keys, because a PR can change `config.yaml`.\n\n```\n.ard/\n├── config.yaml          # global settings and overrides only\n├── scanners/<name>/     # scan (executable) or prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n└── <issue#>/            # per-issue output and logs, written only by the committer\n```\n\n- A directory with `prompt.txt` is an agent role; a scanner directory with an executable `scan` is a deterministic scanner.\n- A `Dockerfile` builds the role's image; without one the local executor runs a host subprocess and the Kubernetes executor uses the default `ard-agent` image (agent CLIs, git, common linters). A role that reads untrusted input without one triggers the host-mode warning (see Executors and the Reaper).\n- Any other file in the directory (scripts, allowlists) is mounted read-only beside the prompt.\n\n```yaml\nmax_pods: 3                  # job slots shared by every loop\nexecutor: local              # local | k8s\nagent: claude                # default CLI for agent roles\nrequire_approval: true       # human adds ard:approved before a worker starts\nbudget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo, UTC day, blocks at spent >= cap; a role may set its own; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2\ninternal/reviewer/doc.go:54:// ard:tests:failed is set. It logs review.marked_ready; a failed MarkReady is\ninternal/reviewer/loop.go:47:\tMarkReady(ctx context.Context, n int) error\ninternal/reviewer/ready.go:86:\t\tcovered, _, err := prgate.Covers(ctx, d.Git, d.RepoDir, head, pr.HeadSHA, \"\", issue)\ninternal/reviewer/ready.go:96:\tif err := d.Effects.MarkReady(ctx, prNumber); err != nil {\ninternal/reviewer/ready.go:97:\t\tt.log.Warn(\"review.mark_ready_failed\", \"pr\", prNumber, \"issue\", issue, \"error\", err)\ninternal/reviewer/ready.go:101:\t\tt.log.Info(\"review.would_mark_ready\", \"pr\", prNumber, \"issue\", issue, \"head\", pr.HeadSHA)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015jBiStijLfymkrM1MjBBDS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
